# NOTES
# Root cause confirmed in codebase: scripts/populate_reactor_details.py, function determine_rpv_manufacturer()
# (~line 1322-1324) hardcodes `if country == 'Japan': return 'Japan Steel Works (JSW)'` for EVERY Japanese
# reactor regardless of unit/design, with its own comment admitting "IHI and MHI also involved." This means
# every rpv_manufacturer=JSW value in this batch (and likely the whole Japan fleet) is a country-level
# default, not a per-reactor sourced fact. Recommend treating ALL Japan rpv_manufacturer=JSW values
# database-wide as UNVERIFIABLE-by-construction until sourced individually, not just the 31 in this batch.
#
# Similarly, scripts/fix_japan_details.py encodes a "vertical integration" heuristic: architect_engineer
# and turbine_supplier are mechanically set equal to whatever constructor value already existed in the DB,
# without independent per-unit sourcing (see file header rationale, ~lines 14-21). Where constructor itself
# is confirmed by a real source (as at Fukushima Daini and most Kashiwazaki Kariwa units), that gives
# reasonable confidence constructor is right, but architect_engineer/turbine_supplier still generally
# lack independent confirmation — Wikipedia unit tables for these plants typically carry only a
# "reactor supplier"/"architecture" column, not separate turbine or AE columns, which is why so many
# turbine_supplier fields land UNVERIFIABLE even where constructor is solidly CONFIRMED.
#
# Two confirmed unit-level breaks in the heuristic's "one vendor per plant" assumption (exactly the failure
# mode flagged in the task brief):
#  - Hamaoka unit 5: claimed constructor "Toshiba" is WRONG — Wikipedia's 2006 turbine-incident coverage
#    identifies Hitachi as the NSSS manufacturer, breaking the "all Hamaoka units = Toshiba" pattern the
#    heuristic assumed.
#  - Kashiwazaki Kariwa unit 6 (ABWR): claimed constructor "Toshiba" (sole) is WRONG — Wikipedia's plant
#    table lists unit 6 (like unit 7) as a three-way joint Hitachi/Toshiba/GE build, consistent with the
#    ABWR being co-developed by all three vendors. The single-constructor-field schema cannot cleanly
#    represent this; flagging for schema owners to consider a multi-vendor field for ABWR units.
#  - By contrast, Kashiwazaki Kariwa units 1-5 (1/2/3=Toshiba, 4/5=Hitachi) and Fukushima Daini units 1-4
#    (alternating Toshiba/Hitachi/Toshiba/Hitachi) were CONFIRMED exactly as claimed against Wikipedia's
#    per-unit reactor-supplier tables — so the original heuristic did get some unit-level splits right,
#    likely because whoever built the original DB entries for these two plants already consulted per-unit
#    sources (only turbine_supplier/rpv_manufacturer were back-filled by the blanket heuristic).
#
# Fukushima Daiichi (6 units, the single most consequential group for exactly this kind of unit-by-unit
# vendor-split check, per the task brief's own example) could NOT be verified in this pass: the dedicated
# research sub-agent did not return results before the batch deadline. All 24 Fukushima Daiichi
# reactor-field lines above are marked UNVERIFIABLE for this reason ONLY (no source was found to contradict
# OR confirm them) — this is a coverage gap, not a finding that the claimed values are correct. Strongly
# recommend re-running verification on Fukushima Daiichi specifically in a follow-up batch; the well-known
# public record (NS Energy, TEPCO, Wikipedia's unit table) likely resolves per-unit constructor/AE/turbine
# with HIGH confidence, and the known GE/Toshiba joint-construction units (commonly cited as units 2 and 6)
# were never checked against the claimed sole-vendor values in the input file.
#
# Fugen ATR and Ikata unit 3 also carry unresolved ambiguities worth a follow-up pass:
#  - Fugen: zero sources found either supporting or refuting "Fuji Electric" for any of its 4 fields.
#    fix_japan_details.py's own comment describes Fugen as a JOINT build by Hitachi, Toshiba, MHI, Fuji,
#    and Sumitomo with Fuji Electric as "lead constructor" and PNC (a government entity) as the actual
#    architect-engineer — if that comment's account is accurate, "Fuji Electric" alone materially
#    understates the true multi-vendor picture, but it could not be independently sourced this session.
#  - Ikata unit 3: Japanese Wikipedia names Westinghouse Electric alongside MHI for the 3-loop design;
#    unclear whether Westinghouse should be credited as co-constructor or only as the original PWR
#    technology licensor.
#
# Tool/process gaps encountered by all sub-agents: IAEA PRIS reactor-detail pages could not be fetched
# (403/404 errors, and the site's ASP.NET postback navigation isn't fetchable by WebFetch); WebSearch quota
# was exhausted partway through the batch, capping how many follow-up queries each agent could run
# (this is likely why so many turbine_supplier/rpv_manufacturer fields ended UNVERIFIABLE rather than
# resolved either way — a scarcity of remaining search budget, not necessarily a scarcity of sources).
